<DocsLayout title="Atomic / Graph-centric Libraries" currentPath={`${base}/atomic-graph/`}>
  <h1>Atomic / Graph-centric Libraries</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground !mt-2">
    Deep dives into libraries where small state nodes compose into derived graphs.
  </p>
  <p>
    These libraries are grouped by state organization, not by pure push or pull semantics. The common thread is first-class derived state and dependency capture from reads.
  </p>

  <h2>Spreadsheet Mental Model</h2>

  <p>A spreadsheet is a useful way to understand atomic and graph-centric systems. Some cells hold concrete values. Other cells are formulas that derive their values from cells they read. When a source cell changes, the spreadsheet engine knows which formulas are affected because it owns the dependency graph.</p>

  <Code code={spreadsheetCode} lang="typescript" />

  <p>The same shape appears in atom, provider, signal, and node systems: <strong>stored nodes</strong> provide source values, <strong>derived nodes</strong> compute from other nodes, and the runtime tracks the edges between them.</p>

  <hr />

  <!-- Jotai -->
  <h2 id="jotai">Jotai</h2>
  <p class="not-prose text-sm text-muted-foreground"><a href="https://github.com/pmndrs/jotai" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-foreground">github.com/pmndrs/jotai</a></p>

  <p><strong>Platform:</strong> TypeScript (React) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Pattern:</strong> Bottom-up atom graph</p>

  <p>Jotai provides atomic state management for React. Atoms are the smallest units of state, and derived atoms are created by composing other atoms via <code>get()</code>. Dependencies are auto-tracked: calling <code>get(someAtom)</code> inside a derived atom registers a dependency. Mounted atoms are then invalidated and notified by the store, so Jotai is more accurately a read-tracked atom graph than a purely lazy pull system.</p>

  <h3>Atom Definitions</h3>
  <Code code={jotaiBasicCode} lang="typescript" />

  <h3>React Integration</h3>
  <Code code={jotaiComponentCode} lang="tsx" />

  <h3>Bottom-up Composition</h3>
  <p>Jotai's key insight is <strong>bottom-up composition</strong>: atoms compose into atoms. State is built from small, independent pieces rather than top-down slices of a single store:</p>
  <Code code={jotaiDerivedCode} lang="typescript" />

  <h3>Relationship to Recoil</h3>
  <p>Jotai was inspired by <a href="https://recoiljs.org/">Recoil</a> (Facebook's experimental atomic state library for React) but takes a simpler approach: no string keys, no explicit <code>RecoilRoot</code> required, and a smaller API surface. While Recoil is no longer actively maintained, Jotai has become a common successor for atomic state in React.</p>

  <h3>Trade-offs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Minimal API, auto-tracking, async support, bottom-up composition, React Suspense integration</li>
    <li><strong>Weaknesses:</strong> React-only, debugging atomic graphs can be challenging, no built-in devtools (community solutions exist)</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <!-- Atoms -->
  <h2 id="atoms">Atoms</h2>
  <p class="not-prose text-sm text-muted-foreground"><a href="https://github.com/ra1028/swiftui-atom-properties" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-foreground">github.com/ra1028/swiftui-atom-properties</a></p>

  <p><strong>Platform:</strong> Swift (SwiftUI) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Pattern:</strong> Atomic graph</p>

  <p>Atoms brings Recoil-style atomic state management to Swift. It uses <code>context.watch()</code> for automatic dependency tracking: a <code>ValueAtom</code> can read another atom, and that read becomes an edge in the atom graph.</p>

  <h3>Atom Definitions</h3>
  <Code code={atomsDefinitionCode} lang="swift" />

  <h3>SwiftUI Integration</h3>
  <Code code={atomsViewCode} lang="swift" />

  <h3>Evaluation Model</h3>
  <p>Atoms is not simply "pull" or "push". Dependencies are captured from reads, while watched atom changes propagate through the atom graph. Values are cached while in use, making it a hybrid atomic graph runtime.</p>

  <h3>Trade-offs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Auto-tracking, familiar atom model, async support, fine-grained reactivity</li>
    <li><strong>Weaknesses:</strong> SwiftUI-only, graph lifecycle and debugging can be subtle</li>
  </ul>

