  <!-- Compose derivedStateOf -->
  <h2 id="compose-derivedstateof">Jetpack Compose <code>derivedStateOf</code></h2>

  <p><strong>Platform:</strong> Android (Jetpack Compose) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Pattern:</strong> Compose-runtime graph</p>

  <p>Jetpack Compose's <code>derivedStateOf</code> provides graph-aware derived state within the Compose runtime. It uses the <strong>Snapshot system</strong> to automatically track which <code>State&lt;T&gt;</code> values are read during evaluation.</p>

  <h3>Usage</h3>
  <Code code={composeDerivedCode} lang="kotlin" />

  <h3>Snapshot System</h3>
  <Code code={composeSnapshotCode} lang="kotlin" />

  <h3>Conditional Invalidation</h3>
  <p>A key feature: <code>derivedStateOf</code> performs <strong>two-level checking</strong>. First, it checks if the tracked source states changed. If they did, it re-evaluates the block. Then it checks if the <em>result</em> changed. Only if the result is different does it trigger recomposition. This prevents cascading re-renders when derived values are stable.</p>

  <h3>Limitation: Compose-runtime Scope</h3>
  <p>The Snapshot system is part of the Compose runtime, so <code>derivedStateOf</code> is normally used close to Compose state reads and recomposition decisions. It is not the usual Android architecture primitive for ViewModels or repositories; those layers commonly use <code>StateFlow.combine()</code> or another StateFlow-based abstraction.</p>

  <h3>Trade-offs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Auto-tracking, conditional invalidation, built into Compose, no extra dependency</li>
    <li><strong>Weaknesses:</strong> Compose-runtime scope, not a StateFlow graph for model-layer dependencies, Snapshot overhead</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <!-- ReactiveState-Kotlin -->
  <h2 id="reactivestate-kotlin">ReactiveState-Kotlin</h2>

  <p><strong>Platform:</strong> Kotlin (Kotlin Multiplatform) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Pattern:</strong> StateFlow graph, business logic</p>

  <p>ReactiveState-Kotlin provides StateFlow-based reactive state with automatic dependency tracking via <code>get()</code>. Unlike Compose's <code>derivedStateOf</code>, it is UI-independent and designed for ViewModels, shared KMP modules, and other business-logic layers.</p>

